 {
  "source": "Goldman Sachs",
  "title": "Oil Comment: Solving for Diesel and Gasoline Shortages",
  "type": "commodities",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-05-18",
  "insights": [
   "OECD commercial diesel stocks are projected to remain above critical levels through end-2026 even if the Strait of Hormuz remains closed, provided higher prices trigger at least two of three 'solvers': diesel yield gains, SPR releases, or demand destruction.",
   "A 1pp rise in US diesel yield typically reduces finished motor gasoline yield by 0.8pp, creating a tightening risk for the gasoline market during persistent disruption scenarios.",
   "US gasoline yields remain robust despite high diesel/jet fuel yields, likely due to the destocking of blending components.",
   "Refining margins must rise significantly to boost utilization rates above seasonal norms, especially given that US utilization is already near physical limits during the summer driving season.",
   "Persistent Strait closure without mitigation would likely push OECD diesel and gasoline inventories to critical thresholds (20 days of demand) by August and October 2026, respectively.",
   "Preventing shortages through end-2026 requires a combination of significant demand destruction (3% for diesel, 4% for gasoline) and either further increases in global refinery utilization or 0.6mb/d of SPR diesel releases."
  ],
  "sector_views": [
   {
    "sector": "Energy / Refining",
    "stance": "neutral",
    "rationale": "The report focuses on physical inventory constraints and refinery yield dynamics rather than equity valuations."
